function COMPONENT(request : dval) {
    print("<div class=\"doc-detail-layout\"><main class=\"content doc-page doc-detail\"><h2>string</h2><div class=\"doc-section signatures\"><h3>Capy</h3><pre>function string(value : dval, fallback : string = &quot;&quot;) string</pre></div><div class=\"doc-section content\"><h3>Description</h3><p><code>string()</code> reads a <code>dval</code> as text. It is the function you use on nearly every request, because everything arriving from outside a unit — query parameters, form fields, headers, cookies, component props, results from another unit — arrives as <code>dval</code>.</p><p>Always pass a fallback when the value may be absent:</p><p>var name := string(request.query.name, &quot;guest&quot;)</p><p>With no fallback the default is the empty string, which is fine when empty is a sensible answer and misleading when it is not — <code>string(request.query.page)</code> cannot tell &quot;absent&quot; from &quot;supplied as empty&quot;. Use the <code>?</code> suffix when that difference matters:</p><p>if request.query.page? { print(string(request.query.page, &quot;&quot;), &quot;n&quot;) }</p><h2>What converts</h2><p>Text returns unchanged. Numbers render as runtime numeric text, and booleans as <code>true</code> or <code>false</code>. Absent keys and values with no text form — maps and arrays — return the fallback rather than raising an error.</p><p>Because a fallback is always available, <code>string()</code> never fails on well-formed input. That is deliberate: request data is untrusted, and a handler should not abort because a caller omitted a field.</p><h2>The other converters</h2><p><code>s64()</code>, <code>u64()</code>, <code>s32()</code>, <code>f64()</code>, and <code>bool()</code> follow the same shape — value first, fallback second — for numeric and boolean fields. Reach for the one")
    print(" matching the type you want rather than converting to text and parsing it.</p></div><div class=\"doc-section params\"><h3>Parameters</h3><div><p>value : the <code>dval</code> to convert</p></div><div><p>fallback : text returned when the value is absent or cannot convert</p></div><div></div></div><div class=\"doc-section\"><h3>Return Values</h3><p>The converted text, or <code>fallback</code>.</p></div><div class=\"doc-section example\"><h3>Example</h3><div class=\"example-language capy\"><h4>Capy</h4><pre class=\"example-source\">print(string(dval(42)), &quot;\\n&quot;)\n</pre></div></div><div class=\"doc-section example\"><h3>Example</h3><div class=\"example-language capy\"><h4>Capy</h4><pre class=\"example-source\">print(string(request.query.name, &quot;guest&quot;), &quot;\\n&quot;)\n</pre></div></div><div class=\"doc-section example\"><h3>Example</h3><div class=\"example-language capy\"><h4>Capy</h4><pre class=\"example-source\">if request.query.q? {\n    print(&quot;searched: &quot;, string(request.query.q, &quot;&quot;), &quot;\\n&quot;)\n} else {\n    print(&quot;no search term\\n&quot;)\n}\n</pre></div></div><div class=\"doc-section example\"><h3>Example</h3><div class=\"example-language capy\"><h4>Capy</h4><pre class=\"example-source\">var target := unit_load(&quot;/tests/capy-module-target.capy&quot;)\nprint(string(target.echo({name: &quot;Ada&quot;}).name, &quot;unknown&quot;), &quot;\\n&quot;)\n</pre></div></div></main><aside class=\"doc-section see-also\"><h3>See also</h3><a class=\"related-link\" href=\"/doc/type/request/\">request</a><a class=\"related-link\" href=\"/doc/api/bool/\">bool</a><a class=\"related-link\" href=\"/doc/api/dval/\">dval</a><a class=\"related-link\" href=\"/doc/api/s64/\">s64</a><a class=\"related-link\" href=\"/doc/api/u64/\">u64</a></aside></div>")
}
